// frame.bs -- binary patterns, and the one inference behind them
// (F13, ticket 30).
//
//     bsc examples/Frame Decode '<<1, 0, 5, 0,0,0,3, "abc", 16#CE>>'  -> :method
//     bsc examples/Frame Decode '<<9, 0, 5, 0,0,0,3, "abc", 16#CE>>'  -> :reserved
//     bsc examples/Frame Decode '<<1, 2>>'                            -> :incomplete
//     bsc examples/Frame Opcode '<<136, 0>>'                          -> :close
//     bsc examples/Frame Method '"GET"'                               -> :get
//
// THE PROPERTY WORTH LOOKING AT is the CALL on the last line of `Decode`.
// `Classify` wants an `Octet`, `t` was never declared as one, and there is no
// guard anywhere -- and it type-checks, because the segment said `t:8` and a
// width IS a refinement. `t` is an integer known to be 0..255 before it is
// passed anywhere.
//
// Compare `wire.bs`, which is this file a feature earlier. There the only way
// to get an octet was to declare one and then GUARD every value on its way in.
// Widen the segment here -- write `t:16` instead of `t:8` -- and the compiler
// refuses the call with `256..65535`: the values a two-byte field can hold and
// an octet cannot. It is reading the width, not the name.
//
// 25c measured the other half of this and concluded the gap was the signature's
// rather than the checker's: without a width, `t` is a bare `int` and an `int`
// is refused into an `Octet`, the compiler demanding clauses for
// `int <= -1 | int >= 256` -- values the wire cannot produce. Inferring the
// width from the segment is what stops every wire function declaring it by hand.
//
// AND THE SHARP EDGE IS DELIBERATE. `Decode(_)` below is not laziness: a binary
// can always be truncated, so a catch-all over one is always legal and never
// reported as discarding cases. Which means if you dispatch on the tag INSIDE
// the binary patterns instead of in `Classify`, the catch-all silently swallows
// every wire value you forgot and no diagnostic will tell you. The pattern does
// SHAPE; the head does VALUE.
module Frame

type Kind = :method | :header | :body | :heartbeat | :reserved | :incomplete

// The refinement a width infers. It is declared here because the DISPATCH
// function's signature needs a name for it -- what F13 removed is the need to
// guard every value on its way in, not the type declaration itself.
type Octet = int where value >= 0 and value <= 255
type Opcode = :cont | :text | :binary | :close | :ping | :pong | :reserved
type Nybble = int where value >= 0 and value <= 15

// VALUE DISPATCH, and the only place exhaustiveness can bite. The residual is
// computed over 256 values because the parameter is an `Octet`; over a bare
// `int` it would be open at both ends and a catch-all would be legal, which is
// how every wire dispatch in this language looked before F13.
private Kind Classify(Octet t)

Classify(1) -> :method
Classify(2) -> :header
Classify(3) -> :body
Classify(8) -> :heartbeat
// The reserved ranges. Three clauses to say "the rest", not 252 -- interval
// patterns landed with the refinements in F2 for exactly this reason.
Classify(0) -> :reserved
Classify(>= 4 and <= 7) -> :reserved
Classify(>= 9) -> :reserved

// SHAPE DISPATCH. AMQP 0-9-1's frame header, which is where ticket 30 started:
// a type octet, a channel, a 32-bit length, a payload SIZED BY THAT LENGTH, and
// a sentinel byte that must be 0xCE.
//
// `payload:size` is the segment ticket 30 §1 permits and refuses to type. It
// runs, and the length is ERASED -- `payload` is a plain `binary` downstream and
// nothing knows how long it is. Every language surveyed refuses to relate two
// fields of one pattern; three of them with a dedicated diagnostic.
//
// `0xCE:8` is a literal segment: it MATCHES and binds nothing. It stays a
// runtime check, and 25c calls it the check that matters most -- catching a
// frame whose length field lies is the dependent step the survey refuses.
public Kind Decode(binary b)

Decode(<<t:8, ch:16, size:32, payload:size, 0xCE:8, rest>>) -> Classify(t)
Decode(_) -> :incomplete

// SUB-BYTE, which is where this design goes past every language measured.
// RFC 6455's header packs a FIN bit, three reserved bits and a four-bit opcode
// into its first byte. `op:4` binds an integer known to be 0..15, and the
// residual over it is computed the same way -- delete a clause below and the
// compiler answers with the missing RANGE, not with a shrug.
//
// Erlang does no exhaustiveness checking at all; Elixir's set-theoretic
// machinery does not reach binaries; Gleam refuses coverage over bit arrays
// even for a one-bit tag with both values named; C# has no sub-byte concept.
private Opcode Name(Nybble op)

Name(0) -> :cont
Name(1) -> :text
Name(2) -> :binary
Name(8) -> :close
Name(9) -> :ping
Name(10) -> :pong
Name(>= 3 and <= 7) -> :reserved
Name(>= 11) -> :reserved

public Opcode Opcode(binary b)

Opcode(<<_:4, op:4, rest>>) -> Name(op)
Opcode(_) -> :reserved

// A STRING LITERAL IN PATTERN POSITION -- ticket 30 §4, the small everyday face
// of the same gap. A string's residual is ALWAYS open, so the catch-all is
// required and legal; a set of literals is never exhaustive on its own.
public atom Method(string s)

Method("GET") -> :get
Method("PUT") -> :put
Method("POST") -> :post
Method(s) -> :other

// AND THE SAME LITERAL AS A SEGMENT, which is the PREFIX match. `Method` above
// needs the whole string to be "GET"; this needs it to START with it, and binds
// what follows. Gleam permits both spellings and treats neither as total without
// a catch-all, which is where ticket 30 §4 took the rule from.
public atom Request(binary b)

Request(<<"GET ", rest>>) -> :get
Request(<<"POST ", rest>>) -> :post
Request(b) -> :other
